narrative:
## Making Everything Printable

A `Display` interface lets any type produce a human-readable string. This is one of the most common interfaces in any programming language.

### Defining Display

```xiom
interface Displayable {
  fn display() -> Str;
}

type Person = {
  name: Str;
  age: Int;
}

fn Person.display(self) -> Str {
  return self.name + " (age " + string.int_to_str(self.age) + ")";
}

type Score = {
  points: Int;
  max: Int;
}

fn Score.display(self) -> Str {
  return string.int_to_str(self.points) + "/" + string.int_to_str(self.max);
}
```

### A Generic Print Function

```xiom
fn print_item[T: Displayable](item: &T) {
  io.println(item.display());
}

fn print_all[T: Displayable](items: &Vec[T]) {
  io.println("--- Items ---");
  let i = 0;
  while i < items.len() {
    let item = match items.get(i) { Some(v) => v, None => continue };
    io.println(item.display());
    i = i + 1;
  }
}
```

### Using the Display System

```xiom
fn main() -> Int {
  let person = Person{ name: "Alex", age: 25 };
  print_item(&person);

  let score = Score{ points: 85, max: 100 };
  print_item(&score);

  let people: Vec[Person] = Vec[Person].new();
  people.push(Person{ name: "Alice", age: 30 });
  people.push(Person{ name: "Bob", age: 22 });
  print_all(&people);

  return 0;
}
```

### Why Display Matters

Without Display, every debug print needs a custom `io.println(person.name + " is " + string.int_to_str(person.age))` — repeated code, easy to get wrong. With Display, you call `.display()` and get the right format every time. Add a new type, implement display, and all your generic functions work immediately.

### The Interface Design Rule

A good interface has ONE clear purpose. Display means 'convert to human-readable text.' If you need 'convert to machine-readable format,' that's a different interface (e.g., Serializable). One interface, one responsibility.

